ion/stats)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Implement MCP stdio JSON-RPC server with tools","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write BDD/GWT xUnit tests covering spec scenarios","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Build, run tests, fix until green","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"6 todos","time":{"start":1782086715342,"end":1782086715343}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":".NET 10 SDK is available. Let me plan the implementation.\n\nThe task is to build an MCP server in C# that provides a knowledge graph interface for Brazilian soccer data. The MCP server should expose tools that an LLM can call to query match, team, player, competition, and statistics data.\n\nLet me design this:\n- A C# class library that loads all 6 CSV files\n- A data layer with normalization (team names, dates)\n- Query services (Match, Team, Player, Competition, Statistics)\n- An MCP server exposing tools via stdio MCP protocol\n\nFor the MCP protocol, I'll implement the JSON-RPC over stdio with tools/list and tools/call. The MCP spec uses newline-delimited JSON-RPC messages.\n\nLet me structure:\n- BrazilianSoccerMcp.csproj (console app, since MCP servers are usually console stdio)\n- Program.cs - MCP stdio server\n- Models/ - data models\n- Data/ - CSV loaders\n- Services/ - query services\n- Tools/ - MCP tool definitions\n\nI'll also write BDD-style tests as the spec asks. Using xUnit with GWT-like structure.\n\nLet me create the solution. Let me build it with a clean structure. I'll keep it minimal but comprehensive.\n\nLet me create the project. I'll make:\n- `src/BrazilianSoccerMcp/` - main library + console MCP server\n- `tests/BrazilianSoccerMcp.Tests/` - xUnit tests\n\nActually for simplicity, let me create a single solution with two projects.\n\nLet me plan the code carefully:\n\nModels:\n- MatchRecord (DateTime, Home, Away, HomeGoal, AwayGoal, Season, Round/Stage, Competition, State info)\n- PlayerRecord\n\nData loaders:\n- CsvLoader (using CsvHelper? or manual). Let me check if CsvHelper is available. I'll write manual CSV parsing to avoid dependency issues, OR use CsvHelper which is the standard. Let me use CsvHelper via NuGet. Actually network access may be limited. Let me write manual CSV parsing with a simple parser handling quoted fields.\n\nActually, let me try CsvHelper first since it handles edge cases. But offline network may fail. Let me write my own minimal CSV reader. The fifa_data.csv has some quirks (numerical columns with trailing values like \"88+2\"). Let me write a robust manual CSV parser.\n\nServices:\n- MatchService\n- TeamService (uses MatchService)\n- PlayerService\n- CompetitionService (standings)\n- StatisticsService\n\nTeam name normalization:\n- Strip state suffix \"-SP\", \" - RJ\", \"(antigo ...)\"\n- Map full names to short names\n- Build a normalized lookup\n\nDate parsing:\n- ISO \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\"\n- Brazilian \"29/03/2003\"\n- Use DateTime.TryParse with multiple formats\n\nMCP server:\n- Reads JSON-RPC from stdin, writes to stdout\n- Implements: initialize, tools/list, tools/call\n- Tools: search_matches, get_team_stats, head_to_head, search_players, get_standings, get_biggest_wins, get_avg_goals, last_match_between\n\nLet me write everything now. I'll create one main project + tests.\n\nLet me start writing files. I'll be careful to make it build.\n\nLet me create the project structure with dotnet CLI.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_eeca540a6001JgRMn0y0U7FIDd","sessionID":"ses_1135b46adffe7APFrgclWh2WTn","messageID":"msg_eeca51a12001RBFs3oQuHunC3j"}}
